> 
> The initial problem was a "unable to load libax*" error. In an attempt to
> correct this I copied all the libs from /opt/applix/axdata/axshlib to
> /usr/x86/lib.

em86 recongnizes various X86_LD_... variables - including
X86_LD_PRELOAD.  This allows you to modify libraries locations
dynamically.  I use that with 'netscape' onf Alpha - for example.

I am not aware about this piece of information documented anywhere.
But a quick scan through sources/binaries reveals their presence. :-)

> I then deleted the libs from /usr/x86/lib, added /opt/applix/axdata/axshlib to
> my /etc/ld.so.conf file and ran ldconfig.

This is, obviously enough, wrong loader, wrong ld.so.conf and wrong
ldconfig.  You are running "foreign" stuff and all these things live in
/usr/x86/  (where **I** would also add all this whole opt/applix ... tree
not to mix it up with alpha binaries).

> Applix started and ran beautifully, Once.

This is a bit of mystery.  As actions you described above could not
possibly to cause that you had to do something else as well.

> The next attempt to run it resulted, after a lengthy pause, in the original
> "unable to load libax*" error.

"Lengthy pause" was, most likely, caused by your loader trying, and
failing, to load x86 libraries - as you added them to /etc/ld.so.conf
This had no big chances for a success. :-)

> Subsequent attempts yielded the same error but
> more quickly.

Results of scan were already cached.  Linux does that. :-)

Try to modify your /usr/x86/ld.so.conf instead and after that run 'ldconfig'
from that tree - as root in a case this is not self-evident.

  Michal


