Hi again,

Sorry for repeating myself but it seems it might be useful to summarize from 
the trenches ...

Eduardo Calixto wrote:
> I had installed the Redhat 5.0 in a Personal Workstation (Miata).  The
> version of the kernel is 2.0.30. I want to know if we have a new version,
> and if I can modify some parameters of the Linux to make it faster.

Is your MIATA new ?

It seems we have three problems with newer MIATAs ("newer" meaning
physically new, the model is still called PWS 433a and 500a and maybe even
533a?), in reverse order of seriousness:

1. Older kernels (at least up to 2.0.30) don't find the IRQ for the SCSI
   disks (so it pops up as 0 in the output of /proc/pci).

   A patch for this exists <URL: http://lhpca.univ-lyon1.fr/axp/>.

2. On some Miatas (like, mine :) newer kernels (at least from 2.0.32) don't
   see the SCSI disk at all.

   This is also fixed by the patch for 1.

3. On some Miatas(again, the one of your's truly :) and, apparently from
   the recent mailings, also other machines, the network card just doesn't
   work, at least it doesn't transmit anything at all.  I have tested this
   to happen with the tulip.c driver versions 0.83, 0.88, and 0.89B; thus it
   is more likely a PCI problem.

It may be important that some newer Miata (like ...:) use a new PCI-PCI
bridge chip from DEC called the DC21152 instead of the DC21052 used in
older models?
 
> I compiled the kernel 2.0.33 in a 486/33Mhz machine and it was more
> faster than a PersonalWorkstation 533Mhz.

For me This was a symptom of problem 1. above ... my kernel compilation
time ("time ( make dep; make clean; make -j boot)") dropped a factor 10
(from 30 to 4) minutes when I fixed this bug ...

I hope this is of use to someone, and I *really* hope that a solution for
problem 3 can be found ... I'll be happy to help when my own machine is back 
on its feet again next week (redhat DiskDruid crashed on me during partition 
table update so now the disk is beyond reach from AlphaBIOS or anywhere else 
untilo it has been replaced (!)).

Cheers,
	Kristoffer

