Maurice Hilarius wrote:

> With regards to your message at 03:10 AM 05-08-98 -0500, Wes Bauske. Where
> you stated:
> >Maurice Hilarius wrote:
> >
> >> With regards to your message at 10:07 PM 05-07-98 -0500, Jiann-Ming Su.
> >> Where you stated:
> >> >Will the Matrox Millenium (not II) and Adaptec 2940 (not W or UW) work
> >> >with the LX and SX boards?  I know this is a bit off topic, but the
> >> >digital's home page showed that not all possible combinations will work --
> >> >especially for NT.  I'm wondering if anybody has had any experience pro
> >> >or con with this equipment.  A friend of mine and I are considering
> switching
> >> >to Alpha and would like to keep some of the old hardware.  Thanks.
> >> >
> >> They will, but I seriously suggest considering planning for a better SCSI
> >> controller in the long run. Narrow, non-ultra disks are 8 times slower than
> >> state-of-the-art Ultra-2 products.
> >> If what you plan to do is VERY non-disk-intensive, then fine.
> >
> >That's a bit harsh. Unless you're into RAID and use 10K RPM disks,
> >the 2940 will work reasonably well. Notice they are trying to minimize
> >costs of switching to Alpha. That probably means the disks aren't
> >high end. For most people, myself included, a narrow SCSI is sufficient
> >for most desktop work. Not all, of course. I use mostly wides for
> >SCSI but I run one Alpha with two narrow cards in it and it works
> >well, no complaints and a PPro with a single narrow SCSI and it
> >too does a reasonable job. Depending on your disks, you get from
> >4-7MB/second. If you need a server, then go with a better SCSI
> >card, preferably UW. How many folks out there have U2W cards
> >and have them working?? I've been thinking about getting one
> >since it allows longer cabling.
> >
> As long as they don't go and buy a narrow disk to "upgrade" it. This would
> be then trapping them.
> Simply put, I think the only reason for buying an Alpha and running Linux
> on it is for speed.
> If you then choke it with slow peripherals, exactly what is the point?

 Bottle necks are relative. What applications are you running on your
desktop that need more than 7MB/sec? The average UW disk is
only good for around 8MB/sec. So, you only lose a MB/sec.
I've used IBM/Quantum/Micropolis/WD/Seagate drives and only
the very latest HH 9GB and higher drives seriously exceed the speed
of a 2940. Servers are a different story. I use multi-GB files and
can easily live with 7MB/sec. Last one I messed with was 12GB
and if you do the math, it only takes half an hour to read it in at
7MB/sec. Assuming you do FP calculations on that data, it's not
hard to come up with half an hour of computation. Course what I
do actually combines the data into a much smaller output volume.
But, even so, I doubt many people are screwing with large files,
particularly since it's only been supported since about 2.1.92 so
all you're looking at for most folks is 2GB, tops which is about
5 minutes to read, and another 5 minutes to write.

As to whether Alpha is a desktop box. If that's not what DEC intended
for the 164SX, it would truly surprise me. If you've listened to this
mailing list, there is a strong interest in office suites and browsers.
I too would like both to be available. As it is, using em86 is an option,
but it sounds like serious performance degradation. Now you have
to say THAT is a CPU bottleneck.... even on an Alpha.

My $.02.

Wes


