Dana Canfield wrote:

> for this, because I know of about 15 Alphas that are being sent back to various vendors
> to be replaced by "stable" Intel machines.  I'd hate to see more go the same way.

We're not talking about a broken OS here. We're talking about broken
applications. Anyone on this list will tell you that picking up a verion
that is 'x.0' is the bleeding edge. In general, those people interested
in 64 bit computing are the types of folks who are willing to work thru
the problems that are guaranteed to exist relating to that situation.

If that's not what you were expecting, you're being a bit unrealistic
about how SW works. The int/pointer problems are in general
minor from what I've seen and it usually doesn't take long to
fix them but you do need programming skill to work on it and a
64 bit system to test on. I've seen a couple cases where the SW
design was really bad and required serious re-writes though.

From what I see, most people have stable systems running on
Alphas. If they are running RH5.0, they've applied the errata to
get the odd problems resolved. Given the number of programs
in the distribution, I still consider the errata list to be relatively
small. Also, note that most of the problems are for both Intel
and Alpha, not just Alpha. So, the majority of issues with RH5.0 are
not Alpha specific.

Good luck with whatever direction you choose.

Wes


