Gary A. Allen wrote:

> My machine is an AlphaPC 164LX running at 533 Mhz.  The original installation
> is Red Hat 5.0. I'm running Linux 2.0.32 .  My SCSI card is an ITIpci SCSI and
> Ethernet host adapter.  I have two SCSI hard disks and a SCSI CD-ROM.
>
> The problem:
>
> When I do a hardware restart, my SCSI bus gets restarted three times, viz.
> once for the AlphaBIOS, once by MILO and once again by the kernel.  Each
> restart of the SCSI bus involves searching for the various SCSI devices.
> These multiple restarts of the SCSI bus is the most time consuming part of
> rebooting the system.  It seems to me that the SCSI bus should only go
> through one restart during bootup.
>
> Question:
>
> Does anyone have a clue about how to deactivate these unnecessary SCSI bus
> restarts?

Don't think you can do that.  Each piece of software does it's own
device scan. They don't talk to each other. So, each has to find out
on it's own what's there. Not sure though if a reset is necessary
to do the scan but the assumption would be to get the devices
into a known state. Warm boots and such come to mind as issues.
I see about a 15 second delay for each reset. Not too bad. The new
2.1.xx kernels have a setting for reset time, at least in the aic7xxxx.c
driver and it now defaults to 5 seconds so maybe it will improve
when milo get's built against that series of kernels.

Wes


