>>> Steve Frampton said:
> 
> I need to get X working under Red Hat 5.0.  I've got probably one of
> the oldest, crappiest Alpha systems around -- trying to get parts for
> it or trying to get information for it is like pulling teeth!
> 
> It's an Alpha XL 233.  Model #A20WW.  S/N #KA543ERFPN
> 
> When I go to run X, I get:
> 
> Fatal server error:
> xf86EnableIOPorts: Failed to set IOPL for I/O
> 
> (I get a similar error when using /sbin/clock)
> 
> I've done symlinks:
> 
> ln -s XL /etc/alpha_systype (no effect or crash)
> ln -s xl /etc/alpha_systype (no effect)
> ln -s Avanti /etc/alpha_systype (CRASH!)
> ln -s Alcor /etc/alpha_systype (CRASH!)
> 
> I've downloaded all the updates and applied them.
> 
> I had this working back in January but had to wipe the system and
> reinstall from scratch and can't seem to do so now. :-(

I believe your XL 233 is one of the "true" XLs, and CANNOT use a real AVANTI
kernel because of certain hardware limitations. There are a couple of "true"
XL kernels available, IIRC, on gatekeeper.dec.com:

  /pub/DEC/Linux-Alpha/Kernels/xl-2030-fixed.gz

is the oldest, but first available XL kernel that fixed a problem encountered
only when a true XL was equipped with a Matrox Millennium.

  /pub/DEC/Linux-Alpha/Kernels/Test-pre34-11b/XL.gz

is the latest and (hopefully, IMHO) greatest, and should work at least as
well as the older one.

Oh, and this:

	ln -s Avanti /etc/alpha_systype

is really the correct one; run the right kernel, I believe, and it'll all
"just work"... ;-}

Good luck.

--Jay++

